      "id": 1,
      "claim": "Sociobiology studies how natural selection shapes social and sexual behaviors that affect reproductive fitness.",
      "evidence": "Foundations of sociobiology (e.g., Wilson 1975) define the field as the evolutionary analysis of behavior.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Paraphilic disorders are diagnosed more frequently in men than in women.",
      "evidence": "Epidemiological data from DSM‑5, Kinsey reports, and the National Survey of Sexual Health show a clear male bias in prevalence.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Evolutionary theory predicts that male reproductive strategy favors a lower threshold for sexual arousal and greater responsiveness to novel sexual stimuli to maximize mating opportunities.",
      "evidence": "Research on the Coolidge effect, the male variability hypothesis, and Buss (1995) demonstrates heightened male interest in novel sexual cues as an adaptive trait.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Sociobiologists therefore view the male‑biased prevalence of paraphilias as an over‑expression of this evolved male tendency toward novel sexual cues.",
      "evidence": "Sociobiological literature (e.g., Wilson 1975; Buss 1995) interprets atypical sexual interests as a by‑product of male sexual flexibility selected for in ancestral environments.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Option (B) – “evolutionary selection has favored the development of males who respond to novel sexual stimuli” – exactly matches the sociobiological explanation.",
      "evidence": "The wording of (B) aligns directly with the conclusion of step 4.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "B",
  "confidence": 0.97,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "A",
      "reason_rejected": "Paraphilias do not guarantee species survival; many are maladaptive or neutral, so this claim is not supported by sociobiology."
    },
